# Normalise line endings.
#
# The build machine is Windows and GitHub runs Linux. Without this, files committed
# from Windows carry CRLF endings, and a shell script with CRLF fails on Linux
# with a confusing "bad interpreter" error. Storing everything as LF in the
# repository removes a whole class of works-on-my-machine problem.
#
# It matters more here than usual because this repository is the database: the
# stored cards are text files, and their diffs should stay clean and comparable
# whatever machine wrote them.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Always LF, regardless of platform: these are read by Linux tooling.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf

# Never touched: binary formats git must not try to normalise.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary

# Never touched: the evidence.
#
# The archive holds what a source actually served, and the frozen fixtures hold
# the same for the two pages the extraction is tested against. Both are checked
# character by character: a stored quote has to be found in them word for word,
# on every push and again whenever a record is rebuilt.
#
# The rule above would rewrite their line endings, and that is not a cosmetic
# change here. Measured: a 54 byte capture was stored as 52 and came back from a
# fresh clone as 52, and a quote spanning a line break was found in the original
# and not in the clone. A quote would then verify on the machine that captured it
# and fail everywhere else.
data/raw/**       -text
tests/fixtures/** -text
